#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "dcd503a8-a9c6-524b-a8ac-0641ce3e1107" :question/math-academy-id "e-12423" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n- $(-2,0)$\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(2,0)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0),$ $(0,0),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "964efb4a-6e6b-5fc1-a993-20aa2b0acdaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-12424" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2),$ and $(-2,4).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "4aa087bf-ee6c-5e90-9431-c4be6b207333" :question/math-academy-id "e-12438" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ca82933-661e-5f97-a835-1e5e885d7be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/4966000e14a3ed4070b9875848c52ae6a4e3e7d01d15aaf312ae0a2119daf042.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284105} {:answer/id #uuid "e915a293-6110-5947-a4a9-7d503ede6513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/fee3415af228b80bb5d7c1a68475043fce324234766d243c928fe9eb98e0b900.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284106} {:answer/id #uuid "1eca9c50-5358-5174-a0b1-dbd0fc8b6168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cf336b4522756131aeb6dd9bc48ada6f8b255526b6ae20120dcf40f155b1b95c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284107} {:answer/id #uuid "2b76b6d8-da41-567c-88b1-217a3d69c2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e997a8908870618b2aea4d1461c8fd719a4fc1ba0fde1c025a830abeed53aac8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284108} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad24bb-3a2c-5fc7-8d26-c836599a24cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/bd92e2e12164033952a9b4c6233563af86ef6b9add13b66700ab166ff04821a1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eca9c50-5358-5174-a0b1-dbd0fc8b6168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cf336b4522756131aeb6dd9bc48ada6f8b255526b6ae20120dcf40f155b1b95c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284107} :answer-field/id #uuid "1160794a-84c7-5140-bbc1-ded3cd0dfaa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7076e203-2050-53c1-bc3c-efab42cd73e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147649" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/57941a3ed467617de9d41a2d8239b75060ba90b7a8c8980e5dabd3afb0be8b6b.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-1,1)$, $(-5,5),$ $(-3,3).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/cf336b4522756131aeb6dd9bc48ada6f8b255526b6ae20120dcf40f155b1b95c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee120c51-ad83-5457-8bd8-a6fcbc510d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102212} {:answer/id #uuid "010f9ebd-8bc1-5fb4-8cf9-7872bec9db2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102213} {:answer/id #uuid "055b1bbe-5e9c-58a6-8fe1-f26400043b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102214} {:answer/id #uuid "0967e215-a9e0-5603-bd67-e07fb561833b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102215} {:answer/id #uuid "53a202b2-0c63-5178-876a-bb66ea00d715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee120c51-ad83-5457-8bd8-a6fcbc510d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102212} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8285fd1-5cf3-5d3c-acc9-9aaef2c12a2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f03e3e5-8f9e-5206-8323-aab2c0298778" :question/math-academy-id "q-162206" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8667b4d3-e8da-5b51-86c7-f5069af1dd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102724} {:answer/id #uuid "96de2d77-24db-56d3-87b9-8efb5afe88c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102725} {:answer/id #uuid "e6db357f-700a-5a75-a186-2aa1fa365131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102726} {:answer/id #uuid "1f067af3-e8d4-5366-829b-67c6e031edf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102727} {:answer/id #uuid "69645bba-ebbe-5cdf-afe7-c90944267b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96de2d77-24db-56d3-87b9-8efb5afe88c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102725} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b453d2c-355f-59f3-be5b-1ca89843e93f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87f1b1b7-4122-5900-82fa-45ed68940b46" :question/math-academy-id "q-21061" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/5c0196b13b3f20ba05313a9604e73b13251937b525a12fd5b9997e99f4ae21a0.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-1,1)$, $(1,-1),$ $(-1,1).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/2f8d6ec6c9a71e61180ad04c25d623c40dadfdd623e1e2eaa8458915bcaca2f8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf7717b0-e75c-518c-8cd3-150b96c8cabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/dc5ad781e838e597706600633701871006cd59fd3db60344cf34aaddb4200da8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284154} {:answer/id #uuid "9db75bfc-fe19-51e4-ab27-cf8792f76f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/40f352980b38be63b071b160953eff23a98996dce58c5f616e10958a8a70182e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284155} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7cdc35-c43f-5b8a-adf1-45e56f6e2a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/92beb71e7d09cba01f2eeb82a60ca3b0b3929064e4f16da3af104db8c5eaa8df.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284156} {:answer/id #uuid "34880a8f-4354-522a-8d0d-3ed1d347e87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/ee4a96b2b1106fbd1a5ec222c3f7f2a5e5a9e84c9bfe539aeb29a1918943aef9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284157} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4b8ff3-6f9e-5e75-9a7e-86d7a7b55fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/b4d2150d92ff14a654fb7079a17d7bd75abe721c0d37d4075d5a92635bafc170.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34880a8f-4354-522a-8d0d-3ed1d347e87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/ee4a96b2b1106fbd1a5ec222c3f7f2a5e5a9e84c9bfe539aeb29a1918943aef9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284157} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aeb8c20-4274-5475-9a2f-7e464fc745e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa19c1ea-c0d3-5f29-b074-4ccd448a8f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-21063" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/6501b1a7e9429c786905c11835410111c32ac0337f1ba63bff130d327350316c.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(6,3)$, $(8,4)$ and $(10,5).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/a91af151d3dc0e48aef0eef58012f6c9f72aaff02aafae2d55fe0714c29c96e3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "941fe68c-360e-5231-9e0b-b1f16aa45e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102730} {:answer/id #uuid "e6653adf-5310-582b-9a7f-e8bfa47891b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102731} {:answer/id #uuid "eb96868e-3d3d-5ab9-9603-9d436aab8ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102732} {:answer/id #uuid "ec82b374-f1b9-54bd-b08d-726849d8de0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102733} {:answer/id #uuid "23f0ec45-7c72-5204-989e-d9480d7cea12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "941fe68c-360e-5231-9e0b-b1f16aa45e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102730} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef7b31ff-a0ed-54d5-a623-08d7218155af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a22c71f-e8b7-5c07-9b29-38703fb5f2e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-21067" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(0,-1),$ and $(0,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(-4,-8)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-8),$ $(-1,-2)$, and $(0,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
